## ABSTRACT

This dissertation proposes and develops the science of multiplanetary institutions—a rigorous, generative framework for designing governance architectures capable of functioning across planetary boundaries, under communication latency, during isolation, and in deep partnership with artificial intelligence systems. Version 0.1 established the foundational four-level hierarchy: raw sciences, design principles, pattern language, and concrete instantiations. It proved five core theorems and demonstrated the framework's generativity through six derived institutions.

Version 0.2 is a structural reconstruction. The first version operated under idealized equilibrium assumptions that subsequent adversarial analysis revealed to be fatal to its empirical validity. We identify four critical vulnerabilities in 0.1—the Anthropic Invariant fragility, the conflation of syntactic constitutional rules with mechanistic AI alignment, the oversimplification of rule composition as a standard category, and the instability of the dual-currency model under asymmetric AI knowledge production—and we resolve each through formal extension of the framework.

The principal new contributions are: a Regency Protocol formalizing the transition state when human agents are incapacitated; a Byzantine Agent Constellation formalism establishing detection and containment conditions for adversarial agent subsets; the upgrade of the pattern category **Pat** from a standard category to a Symmetric Monoidal Category equipped with a cost functor mapping composed institutional patterns to their metabolic overhead; a Zero-Knowledge Verification Layer grounding AI constitutional compliance in cryptographic enforcement rather than rule-theoretic assumption; an entropic decay correction to the Knowledge Monotonicity Theorem that formalizes the maximum isolation duration before digital twin divergence becomes catastrophic; a KCC Velocity Cap resolving the dual-currency hyperinflation vulnerability; and two new theorems—the Constitutional Entropy Bound and the Speciation Threshold—establishing formal limits on constitutional harmonization after extended isolation.

We also introduce an eighth institutional pattern family—Adversarial Resilience Systems—completing what we now recognize as a more honest account of civilizational threat. The central claim of Version 0.1 is preserved and sharpened: institutions, not technologies, are the primary determinant of civilizational resilience. What changes is our understanding of which properties those institutions must possess to survive contact with reality.

