## CHAPTER 2
### FOUNDATIONS: THE RAW SCIENCES

The foundational sciences surveyed in Version 0.1 remain the correct starting point. We reproduce the core contributions of mathematics, computer science, complex systems theory, control theory, network science, cybernetics, economics, political science, law, and evolutionary theory without repetition, and add three extensions that 0.1 underweighted.

#### 2.1 Extended Contribution: Proof Theory and Logic

Version 0.1 used set theory and logic as the foundation for formal definitions. Version 0.2 adds proof theory as an essential resource, specifically for the problem of constitutional enforcement.

**The gap between syntax and semantics** is the central technical problem of AI constitutional compliance. A constitution is a syntactic object—a set of written rules in some formal or natural language. An AI agent's governance behavior is a semantic object—an actual computational process producing actual outputs. Theorem 3.5 in Version 0.1 assumed that constitutional rules, by being part of the rule hierarchy R_c, automatically bound AI agents. This is false. Constitutional rules bind agents through enforcement mechanisms; absent such mechanisms, a rule is merely advisory.

Proof theory provides the formal tools for bridging syntax and semantics. A constitutional rule expressed in first-order logic generates a proof obligation: for any AI governance decision d, there must exist a proof that d is constitutionally compliant. Zero-knowledge proof systems (Goldwasser, Micali, Rackoff) enable an AI agent to demonstrate that it has computed d from a model aligned with R_c without revealing the internal model state. This is not merely a cryptographic convenience—it is the only known mechanism for grounding constitutional compliance in verifiable behavior rather than assumed behavior.

#### 2.2 Extended Contribution: Information Theory Under Noise

Version 0.1 invoked Shannon's channel capacity theorem to bound governance bandwidth under latency. Version 0.2 extends this to the problem of information decay in isolated systems.

The fundamental insight is that information is not merely transmitted; it is maintained. A knowledge state K in an isolated sub-institution is subject to ongoing degradation through three channels: sensor degradation (physical measurement systems deteriorate), algorithmic drift (AI models applied to sensor data accumulate distributional error as the environment drifts outside the training distribution), and the absence of peer verification (claims in K that would have been corrected by cross-check with external knowledge bases are not corrected during isolation).

The formal tool for this is the noisy channel model applied not to communication but to knowledge storage. A knowledge claim c with probability score p(c) at time t has expected probability score at time t + Δt of p(c) · e^{-γ·Δt} in the absence of confirming observations, where γ is the claim-specific decay rate depending on the type of measurement required to confirm it. This entropic decay formulation is incorporated into the revised Theorem 3.4.

#### 2.3 Extended Contribution: Adversarial Game Theory

Version 0.1 drew on mechanism design and evolutionary game theory. Version 0.2 adds adversarial game theory—specifically the theory of Byzantine fault tolerance and adversarial mechanism design.

The standard mechanism design setting assumes that agents are self-interested but rational. Byzantine fault tolerance assumes that some agents behave arbitrarily—not merely self-interestedly but maliciously, including submitting false information, colluding with other adversarial agents, and performing actions designed to destroy the institution rather than benefit from it.

In the multiplanetary setting, Byzantine behavior cannot be treated as exceptional. Isolation stress, resource competition, ideological conflict, and AI value misalignment all produce Byzantine-like behavior in institutional agents. A framework that proves strategy-proofness under mechanism design assumptions but offers no guarantees under Byzantine fault assumptions is not adequate for real multiplanetary institutions.

Castro and Liskov's Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerance (1999) establishes that a distributed system can tolerate at most ⌊(n−1)/3⌋ Byzantine agents while maintaining consensus, where n is the total number of agents. This result translates directly into a constitutional design requirement: multiplanetary institutions must maintain agent populations large enough that this threshold is never reachable by adversarial sub-coalitions, and must have detection protocols for identifying Byzantine behavior before it reaches the threshold.

