**Definition 8.7 (Civilizational Byzantine Resilience).** A civilizational system C has *civilizational Byzantine resilience* at level k if, for any k-subset of agents across all institutions behaving Byzantine, the civilizational system maintains completeness. This is a stronger condition than institutional Byzantine resilience: adversarial agents may coordinate across institutional boundaries, targeting multiple institutions simultaneously with coordinated but individually sub-threshold Byzantine behavior.

**Proposition 8.3 (Cross-Institutional Byzantine Coordination).** If Byzantine agents in institution Iⱼ coordinate with Byzantine agents in institution Iₖ, the combined sub-constellation may exceed the Byzantine threshold of either institution individually while remaining below threshold in each institution separately.

*Proof sketch.* Suppose |B_j| ≤ ⌊(|A_j| − 1)/3⌋ and |B_k| ≤ ⌊(|A_k| − 1)/3⌋. Individually, neither sub-constellation can compromise its institution. But if I_j depends on I_k (Definition 8.3), then Byzantine agents in I_k can corrupt the inputs to I_j, causing I_j to make unconstitutional decisions even though all agents within I_j are honest. This is a supply-chain Byzantine attack: the Byzantine behavior is in the inputs, not in the institution itself. □

**Remark 8.1.** Proposition 8.3 motivates the ARI's cluster-wide mandate: Byzantine resilience must be analyzed at the civilizational level, not just the institutional level. The dependency graph G_C identifies which inter-institutional dependencies create supply-chain Byzantine attack vectors, and these must be protected by inter-institutional ZK verification of inputs—requiring that any institution receiving critical inputs from another institution receive those inputs accompanied by ZK compliance proofs.

